var GAME_RANKS = [
["ancient-egypt","New Kingdom Egypt","Ancient Egypt",1,[["Nesu (The pharaoh)","hem (Majesty), per-aa (pharaoh)",1,"The king stood apart from all other Egyptians.","The pharaoh was Egypt’s ruler, who led the army, made the laws and was honoured almost like a god.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hatshepsut.jpg",1,"CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seated_Statue_of_Hatshepsut_MET_Hatshepsut2012_(cropped).jpg"],["Seru (High officials)","tjaty (vizier), ser (official), pat (the elite)",2,"Below the king stood the small circle of officials who ran his government, the elite that Egyptian texts called the pat.","Officials such as the vizier ran Egypt for the pharaoh, keeping its records and judging its court cases.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-2.jpg",2,"Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_vizier_Paser%2C_19th_dynasty%2C_reign_of_Ramesses_II%2C_1279-1213_BCE%3B_National_Museum_of_Egyptian_Civilization%2C_Cairo_(1)_(52087322624).jpg"],["Ihwty (Farmers)","nemhu (free private people)",7,"Most Egyptians were farmers living in mud-brick villages along the Nile.","Most Egyptians were farmers who grew wheat, barley and flax in fields watered by the Nile’s yearly flood.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-7.jpg",7,"Charles Wilkinson · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sennedjem_and_Iineferti_in_the_Fields_of_Iaru_MET_DT257167.jpg"],["Bak (Enslaved people)","hem, sekeru-ankh (living captives)",8,"The wars of the New Kingdom made it Egypt’s great age of foreign slavery.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were prisoners taken in war.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-8.jpg",8,"Rob Koopman · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asiatic_captive_depicted_in_Tomb_of_Horemheb_in_Saqqara.png"]]],
["ptolemaic-egypt","Ptolemaic Egypt","Ptolemaic Egypt",1,[["Basileus (The king)","pharaoh",1,"The king was a Macedonian Greek.","The king was a Greek from Macedonia who ruled Egypt as its pharaoh from the great city of Alexandria.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ptolemy-i-soter.jpg",1,"Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_I_Soter_Louvre_Ma849_A.jpg"],["The court and royal officials","strategos (governor of a nome), oikonomos (steward of a nome), komarches (village administrator)",2,"Around the king stood a Greek court and a complex bureaucracy built to extract Egypt’s wealth for the crown.","Greek officials ran Egypt for the king, and they collected taxes from every province and village.","/images/societies/thumbs/ptolemaic-egypt-2.jpg",2,"Mary Harrsch · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandstone_statue_of_an_official_excavated_in_Coptos_Egypt_50_BCE-25_CE_Ptolemaic_to_Roman_Period_Penn_Museum.jpg"],["Klerouchoi (Greek settlers)","katoikoi (soldier-settlers), epigonoi (their descendants)",3,"Macedonians and other Greeks were the kingdom’s privileged minority.","Greek soldiers were given farms in Egypt, and in return they had to fight for the king whenever he called them.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/karanis.jpg",4,"Bernard P. Grenfell, Arthur S. Hunt · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karanis1.png"],["Peasant farmers","-",5,"As before the Greeks came, peasant farmers were the vast majority of the people.","Most Egyptians were farmers who grew wheat beside the Nile and gave much of their harvest in taxes.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/faiyum.jpg",7,"Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MadinatFayyumWaterWheel.jpg"],["Enslaved people","-",6,"Slavery in Ptolemaic Egypt was supplied by war, purchase, debt and, probably more rarely, birth.","Some people were enslaved, many of them brought from Syria and sold, and some tried to run away.","/images/societies/thumbs/ptolemaic-egypt-8.jpg",8,"Walters Art Museum · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_-_Slave_-_Walters_542372_1.jpg"]]],
["benin","Benin","the Kingdom of Benin",0,[["Oba (The king)","Omo n'Oba",1,"The Oba was a sacred king.","The Oba was Benin’s king, and his people believed he was sacred.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ozolua.jpg",1,"GuillaumeG · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oba_Ozolua_-_MCN_4185_(cropped2).jpg"],["Uzama (Hereditary chiefs)","",2,"The Uzama were the senior hereditary chiefs of Benin, and their titles passed from father to son.","The Uzama were chiefs who inherited their titles and crowned each new Oba.","/images/societies/thumbs/benin-2.jpg",2,"Andreas Franzkowiak · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MARKK_C3555_Relief_Plaque_with_Dignitary_DSC-1084.jpg"],["Eghaevbo n'Ogbe (Palace chiefs)","",3,"Oba Ewuare is credited with creating the palace chiefs, the Eghaevbo n’Ogbe, as one of two orders of chiefs appointed by the Oba and answerable to him.","Palace chiefs looked after the Oba’s treasures and household, and the Oba chose them himself.","/images/societies/thumbs/benin-3.jpg",3,"Unknown author · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaque-_Court_Official_MET_1991.17.26.jpeg"],["Free villagers","",5,"Most of the Oba’s subjects were free farmers in the villages outside the capital, Edo-speaking in the heartland and of many other peoples in the conquered lands.","Most people in Benin were farmers who lived in villages led by their elders.","/images/societies/thumbs/benin-6.jpg",6,"Olfert Dapper · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Benin_city.JPG"],["Enslaved people","",6,"Slavery was part of Benin’s order.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were captives from wars.","/images/societies/thumbs/benin-7.jpg",7,"The Portable Antiquities Scheme / The Trustees of the British Museum · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Post_Medieval_manilla_(FindID_453400).jpg"]]],
["kongo","Kongo","the Kingdom of Kongo",0,[["Mwene Kongo (The king)","manikongo",1,"The king of Kongo, the mwene Kongo or manikongo, was chosen for life by a small group of electors and senior officials from among the royal kin.","The king of Kongo ruled from the city of Mbanza Kongo and was chosen from the royal family.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alvaro-i-of-kongo.jpg",1,"Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congoking.jpg"],["Mwissikongo (Royal kin and great nobles)","duque, marquês, conde",2,"The great nobles were the core of the mwissikongo, the ruling nobility of the capital: the royal kin, the electors and the governors of the great provinces, which had once been separate kingdoms.","Kongo’s great nobles governed its provinces, and some took Portuguese titles such as duke or count.","/images/societies/thumbs/kongo-2.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardino_d%E2%80%99Asti._Reception_of_a_capuchin_missionary_by_a_local_ruler._Italy%2C_ca._1740.jpg"],["Mwene (Lesser nobles and officials)","fidalgos",3,"A wider nobility of lesser lords and office-holders lived in the capital and the provincial towns.","Young nobles in Kongo went to royal schools and learned to read and write.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mbanza-kongo.jpg",3,"Thomas Astley · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bansa,_or_residence_of_the_King_of_Kongo,_called_St._Salvador_(M%27Banza_Kongo),_Astley_1745.jpg"],["Free villagers","",4,"Most people of Kongo were free villagers.","Most people in Kongo were farmers who lived in small villages and paid taxes to their lords.","/images/societies/thumbs/kongo-5.jpg",5,"Bernardino D'Asti · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dasti_burial.jpg"],["Enslaved people","",5,"Slavery in Kongo was older than the Atlantic trade.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were captured in wars.","/images/societies/thumbs/kongo-6.jpg",6,"Henry Wallis, after William Marshall Craig · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E2%80%98A_Person_of_Rank_in_Congo_Carried_by_his_Slaves.%E2%80%99_RMG_F2268.tiff"]]],
["dahomey","Dahomey","the Kingdom of Dahomey",0,[["Ahosu (The king)","",1,"The king, the ahosu, was the sovereign of Dahomey, and all the kings claimed descent from the royal family of Allada.","The king of Dahomey lived at Abomey, where each king built his own palace with pictures on its walls.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ghezo.jpg",1,"Forbes, Frederick E. · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gezo_(2).jpg"],["Great officers of the court","migan, mehu, yovogan, tokpo, agan, kpojito, chacha",2,"The king’s court was led by great officers.","The king’s chief ministers ran the courts, the palaces, farming and the army, and each had a woman partner.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/royal-palaces-of-abomey.jpg",2,"Joachim Huber · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abomey_2006_1.jpg"],["Chiefs and royal officials","tokpe (tax officials)",3,"Dahomey’s main political divisions were its villages, whose chiefs and officials the king appointed to act as his representatives and settle disputes.","The king chose chiefs to run each village and officials to count the harvest and collect taxes.","/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-3.jpg",3,"Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%A9cade_MHNT_ETH_AC_450.jpg"],["Free farmers","-",6,"Most people were free farmers and their families: farming was done by most families, for local use until palm oil was exported.","Most people in Dahomey were farmers who brought gifts to the king at a great yearly festival.","/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-8.jpg",8,"Jean Duprez · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Village_traditionnel_de_l'ethnie_Fon_au_Dahomey_-_Jean_Duprez_-_btv1b101110693.jpg"],["Enslaved people","-",7,"Dahomey’s growth went with the Atlantic slave trade.","Dahomey captured people in wars and sold many of them as slaves to European traders.","/images/societies/thumbs/dahomey-9.jpg",9,"Borisghost · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porte_du_non-retour_au_Benin.jpg"]]],
["ashanti","Asante","the Ashanti Empire",0,[["Asantehene (The king)","Sika dwa (the Golden Stool)",1,"The Asantehene, king of all Asante, was enthroned on the Golden Stool, the Sika dwa, believed to house the spirit of the nation: the living, the dead and those yet to be born.","The Asantehene was the king of the Asante, and his throne was the sacred Golden Stool.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/prempeh-i.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prempeh_of_Ashanti_and_followers_(Political_Prisoners)_Mah%C3%A9,_Seychelles.jpeg"],["Amanhene (Paramount chiefs)","omanhene",2,"The empire joined metropolitan states, whose people were Asante citizens, to provincial states, kingdoms it had absorbed.","Paramount chiefs ruled the Asante states under the king and met in his great council.","/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-2.jpg",2,"Unknown author · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_85.200.2_Chair_Akonkromfi.jpg"],["Ohene (Divisional chiefs)","Krontihene, Nifahene, Benkumhene, Adontenhene, Kyidomhene",3,"Below the paramount chiefs stood the divisional chiefs, the highest order in each state’s divisions, which grouped many villages together.","Divisional chiefs ruled groups of villages and gave advice to the paramount chiefs.","/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-4.jpg",4,"Unknown author · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_72.172.4_Figure_of_a_Man_Seated_on_a_Stool.jpg"],["Amanfo (Free Asante)","odikro (village chief), abusua panyin (head of a lineage)",5,"Most people were free Asante, the amanfo, citizens of the metropolitan states, and most of them farmed.","Most Asante were farmers who grew yams and plantains and lived in villages led by a chief.","/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-8.jpg",8,"Unknown author · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kente_Cloth_MET_1972.56.1.jpeg"],["Enslaved people","-",6,"During the Atlantic slave trade Asante was the largest slave-owning and slave-trading state in the lands of present-day Ghana.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; most were captured in wars.","/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-9.jpg",9,"Kurt Dundy · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghana_Elmina_Castle_Slave_Holding_Cell.JPG"]]],
["athens","Athens","Classical Athens",1,[["Pentakosiomedimnoi (Richest citizens)","",1,"At the top stood the pentakosiomedimnoi, the “five-hundred-measure men”, whose land yielded at least 500 medimnoi of grain, oil or wine a year; Solon had defined the class in 594 BCE by wealth…","The richest Athenians were expected to pay for warships and for the plays at the city’s festivals.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pericles.jpg",1,"Copy of Kresilas · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pericles_Pio-Clementino_Inv269_n2.jpg"],["Hippeis (Knights)","",2,"The hippeis, or knights, were the second class: men whose land yielded at least 300 medimnoi a year, enough, in Aristotle’s account, to keep a horse.","Knights were rich Athenians who could afford a horse and rode in the city’s cavalry.","/images/societies/thumbs/athens-2.jpg",2,"Sanjay ach · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_horsemen_South_Frieze_Parthenon.jpg"],["Zeugitai (Hoplite class)","",3,"The zeugitai were the citizens whose land yielded at least 200 medimnoi a year.","Farmers of the hoplite class bought their own shields and spears and fought side by side.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/socrates.jpg",3,"Copy of Lysippos (?) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg"],["Thetes (Poorer citizens)","",4,"The thetes were the citizens whose land yielded less than 200 medimnoi a year, or who had none: smallholders, craftsmen, shopkeepers and labourers in the city and the port of Piraeus.","Poorer citizen men could vote in the Assembly, serve on juries and row the city’s warships.","/images/societies/thumbs/athens-4.jpg",4,"Marsyas · CC BY-SA 2.5","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg"],["Metoikoi (Metics)","",5,"Metics were free people who lived in Athens without being citizens: Greeks from other cities drawn by its trade and wealth, some non-Greeks, and freed slaves.","Metics were free people from other places who lived and worked in Athens but could not vote.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aristotle.jpg",5,"After Lysippos · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg"],["Douloi (Enslaved people)","andrapoda, demosioi (public slaves)",6,"Slaves did much of the work of Attica.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later freed.","/images/societies/thumbs/athens-6.jpg",6,"Marsyas · CC BY 2.5","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_St%C3%A8le_d'H%C3%A8g%C3%A8s%C3%B4.jpg"]]],
["gaul","Gaul","Gaul",1,[["Vergobret (Kings and chief magistrates)","rex (king, in Caesar's Latin)",1,"The tribe, made up of one or more smaller units the Romans called pagi, was the basic unit of Gallic politics.","Each Gallic tribe had a council of elders and a leader, at first a king and later often a leader chosen every year.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vercingetorix.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercing%C3%A9torix_stat%C3%A8re_Gallica_avers.jpg"],["Warrior nobles","equites (horsemen, in Caesar's Latin)",2,"The second honoured class, in Caesar’s account, was the equites, the “horsemen”: a warrior aristocracy.","Noble warriors rode horses into battle and wore metal neck rings called torcs, some of them made of gold.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/orgetorix.jpg",3,"Łukasz \"Wanderer\" Grzeszczak at Polish Wikipedia · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orgetorix-coin.jpg"],["The free people","-",3,"Of the free Gauls who were neither nobles nor druids, the great majority, the ancient writers say least.","Most Gauls were ordinary free people who wore warm trousers and worshipped many gods of nature.","/images/societies/thumbs/gaul-5.jpg",5,"Hampshire Cultural Trust, Katie Hinds · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Age_coin_%2C_potin_unit_of_the_Suessiones_(FindID_856431).jpg"],["Enslaved people","-",4,"Slavery among the Celts was very likely much like that of Greece and Rome.","Some people in Gaul were enslaved, often after being captured in war, and their children were enslaved too.","/images/societies/thumbs/gaul-6.jpg",6,"Anonymous · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesar_venustrophy3b.jpg"]]],
["ancient-rome","Rome","Ancient Rome",1,[["Princeps (The emperor)","imperator, Augustus",1,"The emperor was in name the “first citizen” (princeps) and in fact the master of the state.","The emperor commanded the army, made the laws and had his face on the coins.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/trajan.jpg",1,"Unknown artist · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Glyptothek_Munich_72.jpg"],["Ordo senatorius (Senators)","",2,"The senatorial order stood highest.","Senators were rich landowners who advised the emperor and governed the provinces.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/roman-senate.jpg",2,"Cesare Maccari · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cicero_Denounces_Catiline_in_the_Roman_Senate_by_Cesare_Maccari.png"],["Equites (Equestrians)","ordo equester",3,"The equestrians, or knights, formed the second order.","Equestrians, or knights, were wealthy men who ran the emperor’s offices, money and some of his armies.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pliny-the-elder.jpg",3,"Geoffrey · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Illustrazione_del_Lombardo_Veneto_Vol_3_Plinio_Secondo_300dpi.jpg"],["Decuriones (Town councillors)","ordo decurionum",4,"Every Roman town had a council of decurions, drawn from the leading local families and bound by a property qualification that each town set for itself.","Every Roman town had a council of rich local men who ran its markets, temples and festivals.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-4.jpg",4,"Sergey Sosnovskiy · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuirassed_statue_of_Marcus_Olconius_Rufus_(51596875537).jpg"],["Plebs (Free people)","peregrini (free non-citizens), humiliores",6,"Most of the empire’s free people were small farmers, tenants, labourers, craftsmen and shopkeepers.","Most Romans were farmers, craftspeople and shopkeepers who worked hard to feed their families.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/latifundium.jpg",6,"Boyd Dwyer · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominus_Julius_mosaic_in_the_Bardo_National_Museum(12240864473)_(cropped).jpg"],["Liberti (Freed slaves)","libertini",7,"A slave freed by a Roman citizen became a citizen, something Greek observers found remarkable, but not an equal one.","Enslaved people who were set free became Roman citizens, and their children were born free.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-7.jpg",7,"Gary Todd · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funerary_Relief_of_Publius_Aiedius_Amphio_and_his_Wife_Aiedia%2C_Marble%2C_Via_Appia%2C_Rome%2C_c._30_BC_(28117260474).jpg"],["Servi (Enslaved people)","",8,"Enslaved people were property.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later set free.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-8.jpg",8,"Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dougga_cup-bearers_mosa%C3%AFc.jpg"]]],
["vikings","Vikings","the Vikings",1,[["Konungar (Kings)","konungr",1,"Kingship in Scandinavia began small: around 800 Norway alone had some 30 petty kingdoms, and kings’ names are reliably known only for the later Viking Age.","Viking kings led armies and fleets, gave gifts to their warriors and in time made their people Christian.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harald-bluetooth.jpg",1,"Anagoria · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1200_Harald_Bl%C3%A5tand_anagoria.jpg"],["Jarlar (Jarls)","jarl",2,"A jarl (earl) was the greatest lord below a king.","Jarls were powerful lords, one step below the king, who owned great farms and ruled whole regions.","/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-2.jpg",2,"Colgill · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gokstad-ship---Stern-showin.jpg"],["Hersar (Chieftains)","hersir, goði, höfðingi",3,"Below the jarls stood the chieftains of districts, wealthy landowners who led their neighbours in war and law.","Chieftains led the farmers of their district in war and at the assemblies where laws were made.","/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-3.jpg",3,"NTNU Vitenskapsmuseet · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gjermundbu_helmet_-_cropped.jpg"],["Bœndr (Free farmers)","bóndi (pl. bœndr), karl",5,"Most free Scandinavians were farmers (bœndr), the karlar of Rígsþula, who worked their own land with their households.","Most Vikings were farmers, not raiders: they grew grain, kept animals and met at assemblies to make laws.","/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-5.jpg",5,"Thomas Ormston ( Ormstont ) · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St%C3%B6ng_Viking_Longhouse.jpg"],["Leysingjar (Freed slaves)","leysingi (pl. leysingjar)",6,"A thrall could be freed by the owner, in the owner’s will, or by buying freedom with money earned; according to the sagas the Norwegian magnate Erling Skjalgsson set each of his thralls a price and…","Some thralls were set free, and some were even given land to farm by their former owners.","/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-6.jpg",6,"Erik Werenskiold · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erik_Werenskiold_-_Erling_set_his_Thralls_to_Daywork_-_NG.K%26H.B.05040_-_National_Museum_of_Art%2C_Architecture_and_Design_(cropped).jpg"],["Þrælar (Thralls)","þræll, ambátt",7,"Thralls were property: they could be bought, sold and punished, and the 12th-century Gulating law made anyone who harmed a thrall pay the owner, as for damage to cattle.","Thralls were enslaved people, often captured in raids, who were owned by others and made to work without pay.","/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-7.jpg",7,"W. G. Collingwood · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rig_in_Great-grandfather's_Cottage.jpg"]]],
["byzantine-empire","Byzantium","the Byzantine Empire",0,[["Basileus (The emperor)","autokrator",1,"The emperor (basileus) was held to be chosen by God and to be the living law (nomos empsychos), and his court, whose ceremonies Constantine VII had set down in the Book of Ceremonies, was meant to…","The emperor ruled from a great palace in Constantinople, and people believed God had chosen him.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/constantine-vii.jpg",1,"Wooofer · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_(2).jpeg"],["Dynatoi (The powerful)","archontes",2,"At the top of society stood “the powerful” (dynatoi), a legal term for the senior ranks of civil, military and church office, who usually also owned great estates.","Rich families held the top jobs in the army and government, but they needed the emperor’s favour.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bardas-skleros.jpg",2,"Unknown, 13th-century author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BardasSklerosEmperor.jpg"],["Logothetai (Officials)","asekretis, notarioi",3,"The empire was governed by a large, literate bureaucracy: in Constantinople there were hundreds, if not thousands, of officials at any time.","Officials kept the records, collected taxes and ran the empire’s offices for the emperor.","/images/societies/thumbs/byzantine-empire-4.jpg",4,"José Luiz Bernardes Ribeiro · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodore_Metochites_in_the_Dedication_Mosaic_at_Chora_Church.jpg"],["Georgoi (Free peasants)","penetes",6,"Most Byzantines were peasants, and in the 10th century free smallholders, who owned their land and lived in village communities, were the main pillar of society and of the state’s revenue.","Many farmers owned small fields in villages and paid taxes to the emperor.","/images/societies/thumbs/byzantine-empire-8.jpg",8,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Workers_in_the_vineyard%2C_Minuscule_269_(11th_century).jpg"],["Paroikoi (Dependent peasants)","",7,"Paroikoi were peasants who farmed land they did not own, as tenants on the estates of great landowners, monasteries, churches and the emperor himself.","Many farmers worked land that belonged to rich landowners or monasteries and paid them rent.","/images/societies/thumbs/byzantine-empire-9.jpg",9,"Ken & Nyetta · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Fragment_Harvesting_The_Grapes.jpg"],["Douloi (Enslaved people)","",8,"Slavery remained legal throughout Byzantine history and reached its height in the 10th century, when wars in the Balkans and the reconquest of Crete in 961 brought many captives, and Rus’ traders…","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, mostly as servants in rich homes.","/images/societies/thumbs/byzantine-empire-10.jpg",10,"Unknown illuminator · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_widow_Danielis_goes_to_Constantinople_to_meet_with_Emperor_Basil.jpg"]]],
["kievan-rus","Rus'","Kievan Rus'",0,[["The grand prince of Kiev","knyaz (prince), grand prince",1,"The grand prince of Kiev controlled the lands around the city, while his formally subordinate relatives ruled the other cities and paid him tribute.","The grand prince ruled from Kiev, gave the land its laws and collected tribute from the other princes.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaroslav-the-wise.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YaroslavWiseSeal.jpg"],["Knyaz (Princes of the dynasty)","Rurikids",2,"Rurik’s descendants shared power over the land.","Princes of the same family ruled the other cities of Rus’ and hoped to move up, one day, to rule Kiev.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sviatoslav-ii-of-kiev.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izbornik.jpg"],["Boyare (Boyars)","boyarin, duma (council)",3,"The boyars were the senior members of the prince’s druzhina and the highest nobility of Rus’, second only to the princes.","Boyars were the prince’s chief warriors and advisers, and he rewarded them with land.","/images/societies/thumbs/kievan-rus-3.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radziwi%C5%82%C5%82_Chronicle_(miniature_of_Olga_of_Kiev_received_in_Constantinople).jpg"],["Kholopy (Kholops)","kholop (plural kholopy)",8,"Kholops were the unfree of Rus’: in the 11th and 12th centuries the word covered several kinds of dependent people, and especially slaves.","Kholops were enslaved people: their masters owned them and could sell them.","/images/societies/thumbs/kievan-rus-9.jpg",9,"Sergey Ivanov · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._V._Ivanov._Trade_negotiations_in_the_country_of_Eastern_Slavs._Pictures_of_Russian_history._(1909).jpg"]]],
["feudal-europe","Feudal Europe","Feudal Europe",1,[["Rex (The king)","roi, König, imperator",1,"Kings were anointed with holy oil at their coronation, which set them apart from every lord, and they were expected to defend the Church, keep the peace and do justice.","Kings were crowned in church and ruled with the help of great lords, who promised to be loyal to them.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-ii-of-england.jpg",1,"Gerald of Wales · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_II_Illumination.jpg"],["Magnates (Great lords)","duces, comites, barones",2,"The great lords were the dukes, counts and barons who held whole provinces or clusters of manors, often directly from the king.","Great lords such as dukes and counts lived in castles, ruled large lands and led knights for the king.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-the-lion.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_the_Lion_(cropped).jpg"],["Milites (Knights)","chevaliers, Ritter, ministeriales",3,"Knights were mounted warriors in mail armour, the elite of every army.","Knights were warriors who fought on horseback and were given land in return for serving their lord.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wolfram-von-eschenbach.jpg",4,"Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_149v_Wolfram_von_Eschenbach.jpg"],["Burgenses (Townspeople)","bourgeois, Bürger",5,"Walled towns grew fast from the 11th century, and their people did not fit the three orders.","Townspeople were merchants and craftspeople, and many towns won the right to run their own markets and councils.","/images/societies/thumbs/feudal-europe-6.jpg",6,"Ambrogio Lorenzetti · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorenzetti_Good_Govt_Detail.jpg"],["Villani (Serfs and villeins)","servi, nativi, bordarii",6,"Most peasants were dependent tenants of a lord, and many were serfs, legally unfree, whose children inherited their status.","Serfs were farmers who had to work on their lord’s land and could not move away without his permission.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/villein.jpg",9,"anonymous (Queen Mary Master) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reeve_and_Serfs.jpg"],["Servi (Enslaved people)","theows",7,"At the start of the period slavery still existed.","Enslaved people were owned by others and worked without pay; in England slavery had died out by about 1150.","/images/societies/thumbs/feudal-europe-10.jpg",10,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Costumes_of_Slaves_or_Serfs_from_the_Sixth_to_the_Twelfth_Centuries.png"]]],
["venice","Venice","the Republic of Venice",1,[["Doge","dux",1,"The doge was head of state for life.","The doge was the head of Venice, chosen for life, but the councils kept a close watch on everything he did.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francesco-foscari.jpg",1,"Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Portrait_of_the_Venecian_doge_Francesco_Foscari_by_Lazzaro_Bastiani_-_Correr_Museum.jpg"],["Patriziato (Patricians)","patrizio, nobil homo, case vecchie, case nuove",2,"The patricians alone sat in the Great Council.","Noble men ran the government of Venice together, and their names were written in a register called the Golden Book.","/images/societies/thumbs/venice-2.jpg",2,"Domenico Tintoretto / Jacopo Tintoretto · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tintoretto_-_Portrait_of_a_Venetian_Patrician%2C_68.97.jpg"],["Cittadini (Citizens)","cittadini originari, cittadini de intra, cittadini de extra",3,"After the Serrata closed the Great Council, the citizens (cittadini) were instituted as a middle order between the nobility and the common people, a place for ambitious families of wealth and…","Citizens were the middle rank of Venice: they worked as the state’s secretaries, lawyers and notaries.","/images/societies/thumbs/venice-4.jpg",4,"Cesare Vecellio · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cittadini_o_mercanti_Venetiani_in_Soria_-_Vecellio_Cesare_-_1860.jpg"],["Popolo (Common people)","",4,"The common people (popolo) were the great majority of the city.","Most Venetians were fishermen, salt workers, shipbuilders, glassmakers and other workers.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murano.jpg",5,"Wittylama · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murano_sunset.JPG"],["Jews of the Ghetto","ghetto",5,"Jews had no Venetian citizenship and for long no right to live in the city.","Venice’s Jews had to live in one district, the Ghetto, and wear a red cloth, but they had their own synagogues there.","/images/societies/thumbs/venice-7.jpg",7,"DaringDonna · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_of_the_synagogues_of_the_Jewish_Ghetto_of_Venice.jpg"]]],
["dutch-republic","the Dutch Republic","the Dutch Republic",1,[["Stadhouder (The stadtholder)","",1,"The stadtholder, the “steward”, had once stood in for an absent lord.","The stadtholder, usually the Prince of Orange, led the army of the Dutch Republic, but the provinces’ assemblies made the laws.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maurice-prince-of-orange.jpg",1,"School of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:School_of_Michiel_Jansz._van_Mierevelt_001.jpg"],["Regenten (Regents and patricians)","vroedschap, burgemeesters, schepenen",2,"The regents (regenten) were the rulers of the Republic, the men who governed its cities and through them the provinces: not formally a hereditary class, but in practice patricians, from rich…","Rich merchant families ran the Dutch cities, and their men sat for life on the town councils.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johan-de-witt.jpg",2,"Adriaen Hanneman · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanneman,_Adriaen_-_Johan_de_Witt.jpg"],["The affluent middle class","-",3,"After the aristocrats and patricians came the affluent middle class: Protestant ministers, lawyers, physicians, small merchants, industrialists and the clerks of the large state institutions.","Lawyers, doctors and small merchants could read, bought paintings and had their portraits painted.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek.jpg",5,"Jan Verkolje · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthonie_van_Leeuwenhoek_(1632-1723)._Natuurkundige_te_Delft_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-957.jpeg"],["Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers","-",4,"Lower in status came the farmers, craft and tradesmen, shopkeepers and government bureaucrats.","Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers grew food, made goods and ran shops, and windmills pumped water off new farmland.","/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-6.jpg",6,"Jan Pietersz Opperdoes · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boerenhofstede_met_op_de_voorgrond_de_hereboer_met_zijn_vrouw_en_de_schilder_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-784.jpeg"],["Skilled labourers, servants and sailors","-",5,"Below the craftsmen stood skilled labourers, maids, servants, sailors and others employed in the service trades.","Sailors, maids and workers earned more in the Dutch Republic than in most of Europe.","/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-9.jpg",9,"Johannes Vermeer · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vermeer_Lady_Maidservant_Holding_Letter.jpg"],["Paupers","-",6,"At the bottom of the pyramid stood the paupers: impoverished peasants, many of whom tried their luck in a city as beggars or day labourers.","The poorest people begged or worked by the day, and the towns built almshouses for the old.","/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-10.jpg",10,"Pieter de Bloot · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_de_Bloot_(c.1602-1658)_-_Outside_an_Almshouse_-_2771_-_Fitzwilliam_Museum.jpg"]]],
["ancien-regime-france","Ancien Régime France","Ancien Régime France",0,[["Roi (The king)","Roi Très Chrétien",1,"The king stood outside and above the three orders.","The king lived in the great palace of Versailles and was crowned in the cathedral of Reims.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xv.jpg",1,"Workshop of Louis-Michel van Loo · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_XV,_King_of_France_(1710-1774)_edited_2.jpg"],["Haut clergé (Bishops and abbots)","prélats",2,"The clergy formed the First Estate, the senior order, and its leaders lived as great lords.","Bishops were powerful churchmen from noble families, and the church they led owned rich lands.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand.jpg",2,"Pierre-Paul Prud'hon · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Maurice_de_Talleyrand_P%C3%A9rigord_(1754%E2%80%931838),_Prince_de_Talleyrand_MET_DP148275.jpg"],["Bourgeoisie","bourgeois",4,"At the top of the Third Estate stood the bourgeoisie: merchants and bankers, financiers and tax farmers, lawyers, notaries and doctors, the holders of venal offices, and rentiers living on their…","The bourgeois were well-off townspeople, such as merchants, bankers and lawyers.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/voltaire.jpg",5,"Nicolas de Largillière · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg"],["Maîtres et compagnons (Artisans and workers of the towns)","maîtres, compagnons, apprentis, menu peuple",5,"About a fifth of the French lived in towns, and Paris alone had 600,000 to 650,000 people.","Craftspeople in towns belonged to guilds, groups that set the rules for each trade, such as baking.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancien-regime-france-6.jpg",6,"Laurent Grillet · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atelier_de_lutherie_-_XVIIe_si%C3%A8cle_-_Encyclop%C3%A9die_Diderot_d_Alembert-_Planches_-_T4_pl_XVIII_(extrait).png"],["Mainmortables (Serfs)","serfs",6,"Serfdom had faded from most of France long before the 18th century, but it survived in pockets of the east and centre, above all on the lands of churches and abbeys in Franche-Comté, Burgundy, Berry…","A few French farmers were still serfs in 1789 and had fewer rights than their neighbours.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancien-regime-france-8.jpg",8,"Attributed to Louis or Antoine Le Nain · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Famille_de_paysans_dans_un_interieur.jpg"],["Esclaves (Enslaved people)","",7,"Slavery had no place in the law of France itself: the soil of France was held to make free, and enslaved people brought from the colonies fled or sued for their freedom, and some French courts…","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay on plantations in France’s colonies.","/images/societies/thumbs/ancien-regime-france-9.jpg",9,"Unidentified painter · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Portrait_pr%C3%A9sum%C3%A9_de_Zamor_(vers_1762-1820)%2C_page_de_Mme_du_Barry_-_P329_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_3.jpg"]]],
["russian-empire","Russian Empire","the Russian Empire",0,[["Imperator (The emperor)","tsar, samoderzhets",1,"The emperor was an autocrat: no assembly shared his power, and titles, ranks and privileges were his to grant.","The emperor, or tsar, ruled Russia alone, with no parliament to share his power.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexander-i-of-russia.jpg",1,"George Dawe · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Russia_by_G.Dawe_(1826,_Peterhof)-crop.jpg"],["Dvoryanstvo (Nobles)","potomstvennye dvoryane, lichnye dvoryane",2,"The nobility (dvoryanstvo) was the first estate.","Many nobles owned large country estates and the serfs who farmed them.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexander-pushkin.jpg",2,"Orest Kiprensky · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orest_Kiprensky_-_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%8D%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%90.%D0%A1.%D0%9F%D1%83%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"],["Kupechestvo (Merchants)","kuptsy",4,"The merchants (kupechestvo) were enrolled in three guilds according to the capital they declared, under Catherine II’s rules of 1775, each guild with its own trading rights.","Merchants were traders who paid to join a merchant guild, which gave them the right to trade.","/images/societies/thumbs/russian-empire-4.jpg",4,"Dmitry Antonelli · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitry_Antonelli%2C_Portrait_of_a_Merchant%2C_1829%2C_Russian_Museum_%D0%96-10433.jpg"],["Meshchane (Townspeople)","tsekhovye",5,"The meshchane were the lower urban estate, ranked above the peasants and below the merchant guilds: small traders, artisans, shopkeepers and urban workers who were registered in a town but could not…","Townspeople ran small shops and workshops in Russia’s towns and paid taxes to the government.","/images/societies/thumbs/russian-empire-5.jpg",5,"Karl Hampeln · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seller_of_gypsum_figurines_by_K.Hampeln.jpg"],["Gosudarstvennye krestyane (State and crown peasants)","udelnye krestyane",6,"About as many peasants lived on the land of the state as on private estates.","State peasants farmed land that belonged to the government, and unlike serfs they could not be sold.","/images/societies/thumbs/russian-empire-8.jpg",8,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tula_State_Peasants_5.jpg"],["Krepostnye krestyane (Serfs)","pomeshchichi krestyane",7,"Serfs were peasants owned, with the land they lived on, by noble landlords, and bound to it since the Law Code of 1649.","Serfs were farmers who belonged to a landowner and were not allowed to leave his estate.","/images/societies/thumbs/russian-empire-9.jpg",9,"Alexey Venetsianov · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleksey_Venetsianov_-_%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D1%88%D0%BD%D0%B5._%D0%92%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B0_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"]]],
["soviet-union","the Soviet Union","the Soviet Union under Stalin",0,[["The general secretary","-",1,"Joseph Stalin, the first general secretary of the Communist Party, ruled through the Politburo and the party apparatus he had built.","Joseph Stalin led the Communist Party and ruled the Soviet Union; his orders sent millions of people to prison camps.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/joseph-stalin.jpg",1,"Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg"],["Nomenklatura","osnovnoi spisok (basic list), uchyotny spisok (registration list)",2,"The nomenklatura were the people who held, or were listed as fit to hold, the key posts in government, industry, agriculture and education, posts filled only with the party’s approval.","Party officials ran the factories, farms, schools and offices, and the Communist Party chose every one of them.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/politburo.jpg",2,"Politburo of the Central Committee of the All-Union Communist Party (Bolsheviks) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Execute_346_Politburo_passes.jpg"],["Kolkhozniki (Collective farmers)","kolkhoznik, kolkhoznitsa, kolkhoz, brigadir",4,"Collectivization made the peasants members of collective farms (kolkhozy).","Farmers had to join big collective farms run by the state, and they were not free to leave them.","/images/societies/thumbs/soviet-union-4.jpg",4,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%85%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC%D1%8B_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%8F%D1%85_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%85%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%B8%D0%BC._%D0%94%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0.jpg"],["Lishentsy (The disenfranchised)","lishenets",5,"The constitutions of 1918 and 1924 deprived whole categories of people of the right to vote or to be elected: those who hired labour for profit or lived on unearned income, private merchants and…","People the government called its enemies, such as priests and private traders, lost the right to vote, to study and to join a farm.","/images/societies/thumbs/soviet-union-5.jpg",5,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fruit_and_vegetable_market_in_Leningrad%2C_1924.jpg"],["Prisoners of the Gulag","lagerya (the camps), zona (the zone)",7,"The Gulag, the Main Directorate of Correctional Labour Camps, was set up under the OGPU in 1930 and run by the NKVD from 1934.","Millions of people were sent to the Gulag, prison camps in cold, faraway places, where they were forced to work very hard.","/images/societies/thumbs/soviet-union-7.jpg",7,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulag_prisoners_at_work_1936-1937.jpg"]]],
["mamluk-sultanate","Mamluk Sultanate","the Mamluk Sultanate",0,[["Al-Malik (The sultan)","sultan",1,"The sultan ruled Egypt, Syria and the Hejaz from the Citadel of Cairo.","Many Mamluk sultans had been bought as slave boys and trained as soldiers before they rose to the throne.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/baybars.jpg",1,"14th century artist · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baybars,_Medallion_IV,_Baptist%C3%A8re_de_Saint-Louis.jpg"],["Umarāʾ (Amirs)","amir, amir mi'a (of a hundred), amir tablkhana (of forty), amir 'ashara (of ten)",2,"The amirs were the senior mamluks, the commanders and governors of the realm.","Amirs were the top army commanders, paid with the taxes from lands the sultan assigned to them.","/images/societies/thumbs/mamluk-sultanate-2.jpg",2,"Green fr · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baptist%C3%A8re_de_Saint_Louis_-_Bandeau_I4.jpg"],["Mamālīk (Mamluk soldiers)","mamluk (pl. mamalik), al-mamalik al-sultaniyya (the royal mamluks)",3,"The mamluks were the free soldiers of the ruling caste, men who had begun life as slaves.","Mamluks were boys bought as slaves and trained as soldiers, then set free to serve their masters in the army.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mamluk.jpg",3,"Daniel Hopfer · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Mamelukes_with_lances_on_horseback.jpg"],["Fallāḥūn (Peasants)","fallahin (sing. fallah)",7,"Most of the sultanate’s people were peasants: the fellahin of the Nile valley and Delta and the villagers of Syria and Palestine.","Most people were farmers, and in Egypt their fields were watered by the Nile’s yearly flood.","/images/societies/thumbs/mamluk-sultanate-8.jpg",8,"B. Livadas & Coutsicos · CC BY-SA 2.5","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EGYPT._-_Shaduf_(n.d.)_-_front_-_TIMEA.jpg"],["ʿAbīd (Enslaved people)","ʿabd (slave), jariya (slave woman), umm walad, tawashi (eunuch)",8,"Apart from the boys bought to become mamluks, slaves in the sultanate served mostly in households, and women always outnumbered men among them.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, most of them as household servants.","/images/societies/thumbs/mamluk-sultanate-10.jpg",10,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slaves_Zadib_Yemen_13th_century_BNF_Paris.jpg"]]],
["ottoman-empire","Ottoman Empire","the Ottoman Empire",0,[["Padişah (The sultan)","sultan",1,"The sultan, the padişah or “lord of kings”, was the source of every office and, in law, the owner of the empire’s farmland.","The Ottoman sultan ruled from Topkapı Palace in Istanbul and could appoint or dismiss any official.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mehmed-ii.jpg",1,"Gentile Bellini · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bellini,_Gentile_-_Sultan_Mehmet_II.jpg"],["Vezir (Viziers, governors and palace officers)","paşa, beylerbeyi, sancakbeyi, kul",2,"At the top of the ruling class stood the grand vizier, the sultan’s deputy, with the other viziers of the imperial council (divan), the governors-general of the provinces (beylerbeyi), eight in 1527…","Many Ottoman viziers and governors had been taken from Christian families as boys and trained in the sultan’s palace.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pargali-ibrahim-pasha.jpg",2,"16th century · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_Pasha_(detail)_suppresses_the_Kalender_%C3%87elebi_rebellion_in_1527._S%C3%BCleymanname,_TSM_Haz_1517,_f.248a.jpg"],["Esnaf (Merchants and guild craftsmen)","tüccar",5,"The townspeople were reaya, taxpayers, but the sultans needed them.","Ottoman craftsmen belonged to guilds that set standards for their trades and helped members in hard times.","/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-6.jpg",6,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surname_338b-339a.jpg"],["Köle (Enslaved people)","cariye, esir",6,"Islamic law forbade enslaving Muslims or the empire’s own Christian and Jewish subjects, so the enslaved were outsiders: prisoners of war, people seized by Barbary corsairs, Africans brought across…","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were later set free.","/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-9.jpg",9,"Jan Luyken · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slave_Markt_.tif"]]],
["safavid-iran","Safavid Iran","Safavid Iran",0,[["Shāh","shahanshah, morshed-e kamel",1,"The shah stood at the apex of the state with total power over it.","The shah ruled Iran with total power and built a splendid new capital at Isfahan.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abbas-i-of-persia.jpg",1,"Bishandas (attribution) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12_Abu%27l_Hasan_Jahangir_Welcoming_Shah_%27Abbas,_ca._1618,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art,_Washington_DC_(portrait).jpg"],["Qizilbāsh (Qizilbash lords)","amir, khan, uymaq",2,"The Qizilbash, “red heads” after their twelve-gored crimson caps, were mainly Turkoman tribes of Anatolia and Azerbaijan, sworn followers of the Safavid order, who had won the throne for Ismail I in…","The Qizilbash were warrior tribes in red caps who had helped the first Safavid shah win his throne.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qizilbash.jpg",2,"Circa 1647 · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qizilbash_troops_of_Shah_Ismail_I._Circa_1647_painting,_Chehel_Sotoun.jpg"],["Peasants and herders","-",4,"Pastoralism and agriculture were the twin bases of the economy, and the lower levels of society were divided like the upper: Turkoman tribes bred livestock and lived apart from their neighbours…","Most people in Safavid Iran were farmers who grew fruit, melons and tobacco, or herders who raised animals.","/images/societies/thumbs/safavid-iran-7.jpg",7,"Mu'in Musavvir · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_old_shepherd_leaning_on_his_staff_in_a_landscape%2C_signed_by_Mu'in_Musavvir%2C_Persia%2C_Isfahan%2C_Safavid%2C_dated_1676.jpg"],["Enslaved people","-",5,"Most of the enslaved were captives from the Caucasus.","Many enslaved people were captives from the Caucasus mountains, made to serve in palaces and armies.","/images/societies/thumbs/safavid-iran-8.jpg",8,"Jani Farangi Saz · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgian_woman_from_Isfahan_drawn_by_Jani_Farangi_Saz_for_Engelbert_Kaempfer_(cropped).png"]]],
["han-china","Han China","Han China",1,[["Huangdi (The emperor)","tianzi",1,"The emperor (huangdi), the Son of Heaven (tianzi), was always a man of the Liu family descended from the founder, Liu Bang.","The Han emperor was called the Son of Heaven, and people were not allowed to say his name.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/liu-bang.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liu-bang.jpg"],["Zhuhou (Kings and marquesses)","zhuhou wang, liehou",2,"Below the emperor stood the kings, his brothers, uncles, nephews and sons other than the heir.","Han kings were usually relatives of the emperor, and they lived on taxes from the lands they were given.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/han-xin.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%89%E6%B7%AE%E9%98%B4%E4%BE%AF%E9%9F%A9%E4%BF%A1.jpg"],["Guan (Officials and scholars)","shi",3,"Those who served in government held a privileged place just below the nobles.","Many Han officials studied the books of Confucius and were chosen for being good sons and honest men.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-he.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiao_He.png"],["Nong (Farmers)","",5,"Farmers were the great majority and, in Confucian eyes, the “root” of the state, second only to scholars.","Most people in Han China were farmers, and men had to work for the government for one month each year.","/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-5.jpg",5,"ReijiYamashina777 · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shooting_and_havesting_Sichuan_Han_dynasty.jpg"],["Gong (Artisans)","",6,"Artisans stood between farmers and merchants in the Confucian order.","Han craftspeople made shiny lacquer cups, bronze mirrors and fine silk cloth.","/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-6.jpg",6,"Mary Harrsch · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubbing_of_a_tomb_brick_relief_depicting_salt_production_Eastern_Han_period_(25-220_CE)_Sichuan_Province_China.jpg"],["Nubi (Enslaved people)","",8,"Slaves (nubi) were few, perhaps one person in a hundred, far fewer than in the Roman world.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; in Han China there were few of them.","/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-8.jpg",8,"G41rn8 · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xian_2006_632.jpg"]]],
["heian-japan","Heian Japan","Heian Japan",1,[["Kizoku (Court nobles)","kuge, kugyō (holders of the highest offices)",3,"The codes gave every public post a court rank, from the first rank down to the eighth and an initial rank below it, divided into senior and junior and, below the third rank, into upper and lower.","Court nobles lived in the capital, wore robes chosen by their office and the season, and wrote poems for every occasion.","/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-3.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genji_emaki_Yadorigi.JPG"],["Jige (Court clerks and menials)","",5,"Below the fifth rank stood the jige, the ranks from the sixth down to the eighth and the initial rank, held by the menials of the court.","Below the nobles worked the court’s clerks, musicians and doctors, who earned far less rice.","/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-5.jpg",5,"Tokiwa Mitsunaga · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ban_Dainagon_Ekotoba_-_Crowd_Running.jpg"],["Ryōmin (Peasants)","shōmin (peasants living on an estate)",6,"Under the codes all land and people belonged to the state.","Most people were farmers who grew rice and gave part of every harvest to the estate owner or the government.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rice.jpg",7,"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oryza_sativa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-232.jpg"],["Senmin (Base people)","kenin, shinuhi",7,"The codes divided the population into two castes: the ryōmin, the good people, in four sub-castes, and the senmin, the base people, in five sub-castes, close to slaves.","The old laws made some people “base people”, close to slaves, but by around the year 1000 that line had almost disappeared.","/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-8.jpg",8,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shigisan_engi_emaki_3%2C_everyday_life.jpg"]]],
["song-china","Song China","Song China",0,[["Huangdi (The emperor)","tianzi",1,"The emperor (huangdi), the Son of Heaven (tianzi), was always a man of the Zhao family.","The Song emperor was called the Son of Heaven, and he chose the officials who ran the empire.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emperor-taizu-of-song.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Song_Taizu.jpg"],["Shidafu (Scholar-officials)","guanhu (official households)",3,"The scholar-officials (shidafu) governed the empire, and their households formed the privileged “official households” (guanhu), largely spared labour service.","Most Song officials got their jobs by passing very hard written exams.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wang-anshi.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Anshi.jpg"],["Zhuhu (Landowning farmers)","nong (farmers)",5,"Most Song people were farm families who owned some land, and in 1034 four households in five were rural.","Most people in Song China were farmers, and in the warm south many grew two crops of rice a year.","/images/societies/thumbs/song-china-5.jpg",5,"Zhu Gui / Jiao Bingzhen · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gengzhi_tu%2C_'Pictures_of_Tilling_and_Weaving'.jpg"],["Nubi (Bondservants and slaves)","jianmin (\"mean people\")",10,"At the bottom stood the “mean people” (jianmin), whom the law, largely inherited from the Tang code, ranked below all commoners.","Enslaved people and bound servants had to work for rich families, but there were fewer slaves than in earlier times.","/images/societies/thumbs/song-china-10.jpg",10,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sung_Dynasty_Tomb_Painting_Found_in_Tengfeng_City_6.jpg"]]],
["yuan-china","Yuan China","Yuan China",0,[["Qa'an (The emperor and Great Khan)","huangdi, khagan (qa'an)",1,"The Yuan ruler was at once emperor (huangdi) to his Chinese subjects and khagan, Great Khan, to the Mongols.","The Yuan ruler was called the Great Khan by the Mongols and the emperor by the Chinese.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kublai-khan.jpg",1,"Araniko · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg"],["Wang (Mongol princes and imperial in-laws)","küregen (imperial sons-in-law)",2,"Below the emperor stood the Golden Lineage, the princes descended from Genghis Khan, and the families that married into it.","Mongol princes were relatives of the emperor who lived on the taxes of lands given to them.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kurultai.jpg",2,"British Library · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folio_248._Entertainment_given_by_Timur,_on_his_arrival_in_Samarkand._Zafarnama,_1533_(British_Library,_I.O._Islamic_137).jpg"],["Guan (Officials)","darughachi (overseers)",3,"The Yuan kept the institutions of a Chinese state, a Central Secretariat, a Bureau of Military Affairs and a Censorate, but filled the posts by origin.","Yuan officials ran the government, and the very top jobs were kept for Mongols and peoples from Central and West Asia.","/images/societies/thumbs/yuan-china-3.jpg",3,"Liu Guandao · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_wearing_a_Mongol_robe_with_underarm_openings.JPG"],["Hanren (Northern Chinese)","",8,"The third category, the Han ren, took in the subjects of the former Jin state in the north: northern Chinese, together with Khitans, Jurchens and Koreans.","Northern Chinese farmers ranked above the southern Chinese under Mongol rule.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/khitans.jpg",8,"Hu Gui · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MongolHuntersSong.jpg"],["Nanren (Southern Chinese)","manzi",9,"The southerners (nanren), the subjects of the Southern Song conquered in the 1270s, were by far the largest group and the lowest of the four, because they had resisted longest.","Southern Chinese were the biggest group in Yuan China but the least favoured by its Mongol rulers.","/images/societies/thumbs/yuan-china-9.jpg",9,"Wu Zhen · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Zhen_-_Fisherman_-_1989.363.33_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg"],["Qukou (Enslaved people)","nubi",10,"The Mongol conquests enslaved great numbers of people.","Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many had been captured in wars.","/images/societies/thumbs/yuan-china-10.jpg",10,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Y%C3%BCen_Dynasty_Mural_Painting_from_a_Tomb_in_H%C3%AAng-shan_County_(%E6%A9%AB%E5%B1%B1%E7%B8%A3)%2C_Shensi_Province_(%E9%99%9D%E8%A5%BF%E7%9C%81).jpg"]]],
["joseon","Joseon Korea","Joseon Korea",0,[["Wang (The king)","imgeum",1,"The king (wang) stood at the head of the state and of its Confucian order.","Joseon kings ruled with the help of scholar-officials, and King Sejong gave Korea its own alphabet, hangul.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/taejo-of-joseon.jpg",1,"Jo Jung-muk(?-?), Pak Gijun(?-?), Baek Eunbae(1820-?), Yu Suk(1827-1873) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Taejo_Yi_02.jpg"],["Yangban (Scholar-aristocrats)","yangban (munban, civil, muban, military), sadaebu",2,"The yangban, the “two orders” of civil (munban) and military (muban) officials, were the ruling class.","Yangban were Korea’s ruling families, whose sons studied for years to pass the government exams.","/images/societies/thumbs/joseon-2.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Portrait_of_Heo_Gyo-Joseon.jpg"],["Jungin (Middle people)","hyangni (local clerks), seoeol (sons of concubines)",3,"Between the yangban and the commoners stood the jungin, the “middle people”: the hereditary technical specialists of the government and their families.","The “middle people” were Korea’s interpreters, doctors and clerks, who passed their own special exams.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kim-hong-do.jpg",3,"Kim Hong-do · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kim_Hongdo.jpg"],["Sangmin (Commoners)","yangin (\"good people\")",4,"Most Koreans were commoners (sangmin), the free “good people” (yangin) of the law: farmers above all, with artisans, merchants and fishermen.","Most Koreans were farmers, and by law ordinary people had to wear plain white clothes.","/images/societies/thumbs/joseon-4.jpg",4,"Kim Hong-do · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danwon_Byeotajak.jpg"],["Nobi (Enslaved people)","gongnobi (state-owned), sanobi (privately owned)",5,"The nobi were the core of the lowborn (cheonmin), and in the 15th to 17th centuries, the system’s height, they made up at least 30 per cent of the people.","Enslaved people, called nobi, were owned by others and had to work for them; their children were born enslaved too.","/images/societies/thumbs/joseon-5.jpg",5,"Jocelyndurrey · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EB%85%B8%EB%B9%84%EC%9E%90%EB%A7%A4%EB%AC%B8%EC%84%9C.JPG"]]],
["ming-china","the Ming","Ming China",0,[["Huangdi (The emperor)","",1,"The emperor stood on a social and legal tier above everyone else, embodying a heavenly mandate to judicial and executive authority.","The emperor ruled all of China from the Forbidden City in Beijing, with ministers and palace servants to help him.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wanli-emperor.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MingShenzong1.jpg"],["Wang (Imperial princes)","",2,"Descendants of the first Ming emperor were made princes and given military commands, usually nominal, annual stipends and large estates.","The emperor’s relatives were made princes with big estates and pay, but they were not allowed to govern.","/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-2.jpg",2,"Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince's_Robe%2C_Tomb_of_Ming_Prince_Zhu_Tan_(10144889466).jpg"],["Shidafu (Officials)","",3,"After 1384, when the founder stopped staffing his bureaus by recommendation only, the officials were recruited through the examinations.","Officials who had passed very hard exams on old books ran the government, from ministers to county magistrates.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/scholar-officials.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Jiang_Shunfu.jpg"],["Shenshi (The degree-holding gentry)","shengyuan (\"government students\"), juren, jinshi",4,"Below the officials stood the gentry, landholding families whose men had earned examination degrees: by the 16th century some 100,000 shengyuan, “government students”, the lowest tier of graduates…","Men who passed the first exams became gentry, the leaders of their towns and villages.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jinshi.jpg",5,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%8B%E7%93%8A%E4%BA%8B%E8%B9%9F%E5%9C%96.jpg"],["Nong (Farmers)","liangmin (good people)",6,"Farmers ranked second in theory, after the scholars, as the primary creators of wealth, and the land tax on their plots produced much of the state’s revenue.","Most people were farmers who grew rice, wheat, tea or silk and paid taxes to the emperor.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huai-river.jpg",8,"MNXANL · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:201806_Bengbu_and_Huai_River.jpg"],["Jianmin (The mean people)","",7,"Beneath the four occupations were the “mean people” (jianmin), set against the “good people” (liangmin): those whose work was thought worthless or “filthy”, entertainers and prostitutes above all.","Entertainers such as musicians and dancers were looked down on by law, even when audiences loved them.","/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-11.jpg",11,"Guo Xu · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%9C%E5%B1%B1%E6%90%BA%E5%A6%93%E5%9B%BE.jpg"],["Nubi (Enslaved people)","",8,"Slavery was comparatively uncommon in Chinese history and was practised largely as a judicial punishment for crimes.","Some people were enslaved, often as a punishment for crimes, even though the first Ming emperor tried to end slavery.","/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-12.jpg",12,"Shen Shichong · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shen_Shichong.Man_and_Servant_Beneath_Trees._1616._Collection_the_University_of_California%2C_Berkeley_Art_Museum.jpg"]]],
["tokugawa-japan","Tokugawa Japan","Tokugawa Japan",1,[["Daimyō","",3,"Below the shogun stood the daimyo, the lords of about 260 to 270 domains (han), each assessed at 10,000 koku of rice or more; the greatest, the Maeda of Kaga, held about a million.","Daimyo were great lords who ruled parts of Japan for the shogun and had to live in his city every other year.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/date-masamune.jpg",3,"Kanō Yasunobu · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_DATE_Masamune.jpg"],["Bushi (Samurai)","samurai",4,"The samurai were the hereditary warrior class, perhaps 400,000 men.","Samurai were warriors who wore two swords, and in the long Edo peace most of them worked as officials and guards.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bushido.jpg",4,"Felice Beato · CC BY 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koboto_Santaro,_a_Japanese_military_commander_Wellcome_V0037661.jpg"],["Hyakushō (Peasants)","",6,"Four people in five were peasants (hyakushō), the villagers who grew the rice on which the whole order rested.","Most people in Edo Japan were farmers who grew rice and paid a large share of it to their lord.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ninomiya-sontoku.jpg",6,"Tak1701d · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Ninomiya_Sontoku_02.JPG"],["Kawata (Outcasts of the leather trades)","eta (the official term, now a slur)",7,"The kawata, called eta in official documents (a term now a slur), were hereditary outcasts.","Kawata families did jobs such as leatherwork that others thought unclean, and they were made to live apart.","/images/societies/thumbs/tokugawa-japan-9.jpg",9,"katorisi · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setta%2Cjapanese-leather-soled-sandals%2Cjapan.JPG"]]],
["qing-china","the Qing","Qing China",0,[["Huangdi (The emperor)","tianzi (Son of Heaven), ejen",1,"The emperor was an absolute ruler who presided over the six Boards and held, in Confucian theory, the Mandate of Heaven.","The emperor ruled China and the lands beyond it from the Forbidden City in Beijing.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kangxi-emperor.jpg",1,"Unknown author Qing Dynasty Court Painter · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85%E5%9C%A3%E7%A5%96%E5%BA%B7%E7%86%99%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F.jpg"],["The imperial clan and the Manchu nobility","Aisin Gioro (the imperial house), the Eight Great Houses, qinwang (prince of the blood)",2,"Above the officials stood a small hereditary aristocracy: the princes of the imperial Aisin Gioro house and the Manchu nobles of the “Eight Great Houses”, who held noble titles, as against the…","Princes of the emperor’s family and a few great Manchu families held noble titles and led the banner armies.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dorgon.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dorgon,_the_Prince_Rui_(17th_century).jpg"],["Shidafu (Officials)","",3,"The Qing kept the Ming bureaucracy and divided its posts into civil and military, each in nine grades, from a Grand Secretary down to a deputy jail warden.","Officials who had passed very hard exams ran the government, from ministers in Beijing to the judges of every county.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/scholar-officials.jpg",3,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Jiang_Shunfu.jpg"],["Shenshi (The degree-holding gentry)","shengyuan or xiucai, juren, jinshi (the degrees)",4,"The gentry were defined by law: a man who had passed at least the first level of the examinations held a degree that qualified him for office, and his wife and some relatives shared his status.","Men who passed the first exams became gentry, the leaders of their towns and villages.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/imperial-examination.jpg",4,"Qiu Ying (仇英) (attributed) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming-Beamtenpr%C3%BCfungen1.jpg"],["Nong (Farmers)","liangmin (good people)",6,"In the Confucian scheme farmers stood second, after the scholars, as the primary creators of wealth, and Qing law counted them among the good people.","Most people were farmers, and new crops such as sweet potatoes and peanuts helped feed a fast-growing population.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rice.jpg",7,"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oryza_sativa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-232.jpg"],["Jianmin (The mean people)","",8,"Below the good people Qing law placed the mean people: entertainers, including actors and prostitutes, tattooed criminals and the low-level employees of government officials; the rules of the…","Actors, entertainers and some poor workers were called “mean people” by law and were not allowed to take the exams.","/images/societies/thumbs/qing-china-11.jpg",11,"Adolf-Nikolay Boiarskii · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Actors_of_the_Chinese_Theater_in_Costume._Beijing%2C_1874_WDL1933.png"]]],
["classical-india","Classical India","Classical India",0,[["Rājan (The king)","mahārājādhirāja",1,"The law books made kingship the duty of a Kshatriya: to protect his subjects, punish wrongdoers and uphold the order of the varnas, in return for a share of the harvest, traditionally a sixth.","Indian kings took part of every harvest as tax and were expected to protect their people.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/samudragupta.jpg",1,"PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamudraguptaCoin.png"],["Vaiśya (Vaishyas)","śreṣṭhin, sārthavāha",4,"The Vaishyas were the third varna and, like the two above them, twice-born, entitled to study the Vedas.","Vaishyas were traders, bankers and farmers, and many of them belonged to guilds.","/images/societies/thumbs/classical-india-5.jpg",5,"Unknown 5th-century painter; photograph by Sweekar Bhushan Pamulaparthy · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_Cave_2_three-mast_sailship.jpg"],["Śūdra (Shudras)","",5,"The Shudras were the fourth varna, born, the law books said, to serve the three twice-born varnas.","Shudras were farmers, workers and craftspeople, and old law books said they should serve the higher groups.","/images/societies/thumbs/classical-india-6.jpg",6,"After an ancient relief, in A History of India (1906) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ploughing_-_Page_14_-_Chapter_III_-_History_of_India_Vol_1_(1906).png"],["Caṇḍāla (Chandalas and other outcastes)","śvapaca",6,"Below and outside the four varnas the texts placed groups they held to be impure, above all the Chandalas, along with the Shvapachas and peoples such as the Nishadas.","Chandalas were made to live apart from others and did work such as carrying away the dead.","/images/societies/thumbs/classical-india-7.jpg",7,"Marianne North · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marianne_North_(1830-1890)_-_'The_Burning_Ghats._Benares._India._October_1878'_-_WD3232_-_British_Library.jpg"]]],
["khmer-empire","Angkor","the Khmer Empire",1,[["Devaraja (The king)","chakravartin, kamraten jagad ta raja",1,"Since Jayavarman II proclaimed himself chakravartin, “universal ruler”, and devaraja, “god-king”, in 802, Khmer kings were held to have the divine quality of living gods, incarnations of Shiva or…","The kings of Angkor were seen as gods on earth, and they built huge stone temples such as Angkor Wat and the Bayon.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jayavarman-vii.jpg",1,"Suzan Black · CC BY 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JayavarmanVII.jpg"],["Kshatriya (Royalty, ministers and nobles)","",2,"The kshatriyas, royalty, nobles, warlords, soldiers and warriors, formed the governing elite.","Princes and ministers rode on elephants under red umbrellas, and the higher a man’s rank, the bigger his house.","/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-2.jpg",2,"Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat_-_065_Suryavarman_Procession_(8581685668).jpg"],["Rice farmers and fishermen","-",5,"Rice farmers and fishermen formed the large majority.","Most Khmer people were rice farmers and fishermen who lived in bamboo houses on stilts, safe from floods.","/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-6.jpg",6,"diego_cue · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rice_fields_around_Angkor_Thom_-_Cambodia_-_panoramio.jpg"],["Slaves","-",6,"Slaves stood on the lowest social level of Khmer society, beneath the farmers and fishermen.","At the bottom of Khmer society were slaves, people who belonged to others and were not free.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/angkor-thom.jpg",8,"This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Thom_(II).jpg"]]],
["majapahit","Majapahit","Majapahit",1,[["The king","chakravartin",1,"The king was the paramount ruler: as chakravartin he was considered the universal ruler and believed to be a living god on earth, regarded as the incarnation of Shiva, Vishnu and the Buddha at once…","The king of Majapahit was seen as a living god, and once a year people from many islands came to bring him tribute.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/raden-wijaya.jpg",1,"Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harihara_Majapahit_1.JPG"],["Bhre (Royal kin and provincial lords)","Paduka Bhattara, Bhattara Saptaprabhu",2,"The title Bhre, “duke or duchess”, marked the highest position below the monarch.","The king’s close relatives ruled the provinces of Java and helped him make big decisions.","/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-2.jpg",2,"Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suhita.jpg"],["Mantri (Ministers, officials and nobles)","Rakryan Mantri ri Pakira-kiran, Rakryan Mapatih, Patih Hamangkubhumi, akuwu",3,"Royal edicts passed from the king to the high officials and on to their subordinates.","Ministers helped the king run Majapahit, and the greatest of them, Gajah Mada, swore to unite the islands.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/adityawarman.jpg",3,"Thomas Lehmkuhl · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adityawarman.jpg"],["Rice farmers","-",5,"Most people farmed.","Most people in Majapahit were rice farmers, and their rich fields gave two harvests a year.","/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-8.jpg",8,"Anandajoti Bhikkhu from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:050_Relief%2C_Fields_and_Mountains%2C_Museum_Mojopahit_(40429469961).jpg"]]],
["delhi-sultanate","the Delhi Sultanate","the Delhi Sultanate",0,[["Sultān","",1,"The sultan granted and confirmed the iqtas that paid his nobles, and for many Muslim observers his rule was justified by the protection and advancement of the faith.","The sultan of Delhi ruled much of India. One sultan even made his whole court move to a new capital far to the south.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/firuz-shah-tughluq.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firoz_Shah_Taghlak.jpg"],["Amīr (Nobles and revenue holders)","khān, malik, muqtiʿ, iqtadār",2,"The great men of the sultanate bore the titles khan and malik, and the elite of its early years were overwhelmingly immigrants from Iran and Central Asia: Persians, Turks, Ghuris and Khalaj.","Nobles called khans and maliks collected the taxes of lands the sultan gave them and used the money to pay soldiers.","/images/societies/thumbs/delhi-sultanate-2.jpg",2,"SpeakingArch · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_Tanka_Coin_of_Alauddin_Khilji_circa_1296-1312_CE%2C_Khilji_Dynasty%2C_Delhi_Sultanate.jpg"],["Townspeople","-",5,"The towns held the sultanate’s merchants, craftsmen and labourers.","In the towns, craftsmen and traders made and sold goods, and the spinning wheel came into use in India.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/spinning-wheel.jpg",8,"Unknown author · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_amorous_encounter_-_Smithfield_Decretals_(Decretals_of_Gregory_IX)_(c.1340),_f.139_-_BL_Royal_MS_10_E_IV.jpg"],["Banda (Enslaved people)","ghulām",6,"Slavery became the predominant system of North India in the 13th century, André Wink writes, and kept considerable importance in the 14th.","Many people were captured in wars or for unpaid taxes and forced to work as slaves, and some were sold far away in Central Asia.","/images/societies/thumbs/delhi-sultanate-10.jpg",10,"Pieter Boudewyn Van Der Aa · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melik_Ambar%2C_un_miserable_esclave%2C_Protecteur_du_Royaume_de_Decan.png"]]],
["ayutthaya","Ayutthaya","the Ayutthaya Kingdom",1,[["Phra Chao Phaendin (The king)","chakkraphat",1,"From the 17th century the king of Ayutthaya was an absolute monarch with a semi-religious standing.","The king of Ayutthaya was called Lord of the Land, and people spoke to him in a special royal language.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phetracha.jpg",1,"Gaspar Bouttats, (sr.) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PITERA_TJAY_Rex_Siam_by_Gaspar_Bouttat_1690.jpg"],["Princes of the royal clan","uparaja, Maha Uparat",2,"The king’s kin stood above every official.","The king’s sons and brothers lived at court, and one of them was chosen to be the next king.","/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-2.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Procession_during_King_Narai_of_Ayutthaya_Manuscript.jpg"],["Mun nai (Officials and lords)","amat, nai, chao nai",3,"A service nobility of a few thousand men governed the kingdom.","Each noble in Ayutthaya had a number that showed his rank, and he led the men who worked and fought for the king.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kosa-pan.jpg",3,"Antoine Benoist (painter) (1632–1717) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kosa_Pan_of_Siam_Portrait_by_Antoine_Benoist_1686.png"],["Phrai (Commoners)","phrai luang, phrai som, phrai suay",5,"Every free man was registered as a phrai under a lord, a system Ramathibodi II (r. 1491–1529) made general.","Most people in Ayutthaya were rice farmers, and every man had to work for the king or a lord for part of each year.","/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-6.jpg",6,"Simon de la Loubère · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maison_d'un_Siamois_(Siamese_House)_Loubere_c1687.png"],["That (Enslaved people)","",6,"Slaves, that (from the Pali dasa), formed the lowest rank, with a sakdina of 5.","Some people in Ayutthaya were slaves who belonged to a master, often because they were captured in war or owed money.","/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-7.jpg",7,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slaves_in_Bangkok_1895.jpg"]]],
["mughal-empire","Mughal India","the Mughal Empire",0,[["Pādshāh (The emperor)","",1,"The emperor, the pādshāh, was the source of every rank.","The Mughal emperor gave every noble his rank, and when he died his sons often fought each other for the throne.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akbar.jpg",1,"Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg"],["Mansabdār (Ranked nobles and officers)","umarā",2,"Under the mansabdari system every officer of the empire above the lowest levels, judges apart, held a mansab, a numbered rank in one service that was army, nobility and civil government at once.","Mughal nobles held numbered ranks that showed how many horsemen they had to keep ready for the emperor.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/man-singh-i.jpg",2,"Mughal Artists · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_Singh,_c._1590.jpg"],["Artisans and townspeople","",5,"Mughal India had many large towns: under Akbar, Nizamuddin Ahmad counted 120 great cities and 3,200 townships, and Agra, Lahore and Delhi may each have held several hundred thousand people.","Mughal weavers made fine cotton cloth, such as muslin, that people bought all over Asia and Europe.","/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-6.jpg",6,"Balchand · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balchand._Akbar_inspecting_construction_for_his_new_capital_Fatehpur_Sikri%2C_Akbarnama%2C_1603-5%2C_Chester_Beatty_Library.jpg"],["The lowest castes","",6,"Below the peasants stood the castes that Brahmin texts placed outside the four varnas altogether and that higher castes treated as untouchable, because their hereditary work, such as tanning hides…","People of the lowest castes were kept apart by higher castes and did work such as tanning leather and sweeping.","/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-8.jpg",8,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Chamars_with_caption_'Chamars_cutting_leather_and_making_shoes'.jpg"]]],
["british-raj","British India","the British Raj",0,[["Viceroy","governor-general",1,"After 1858 the governor-general of India was more commonly called the viceroy, from his second role as the Crown’s representative to the princely states.","The viceroy ruled India for the British queen or king, who lived far away in Britain.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/george-curzon.jpg",1,"George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Curzon2.jpg"],["Zamindar (Zamindars and great landholders)","zamindari",3,"The zamindars held the great estates of the north and east.","Zamindars were rich landowners who paid the government a fixed sum each year for their great estates.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zamindar.jpg",4,"Unidentified painter · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehtab_Chand_of_Burdwan.jpg"],["Ryot (Peasants)","",5,"Most Indians were peasants, the ryots, living at subsistence level.","Most Indians were farmers who paid taxes on their land, and millions died when terrible famines came.","/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-8.jpg",8,"Captain Cornelius Francis (Frank) James · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:'Concan_Coonbie'_cultivator%2C_India%2C_1870_(c).jpg"],["Castes treated as untouchable","untouchables, Depressed Classes",6,"Outside the four varnas stood the communities treated as untouchable, the Dalits.","People of the castes called untouchable were kept apart, even at the village well, and reformers like Ambedkar fought for their rights.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/b-r-ambedkar.jpg",10,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr._Bhimrao_Ambedkar.jpg"]]],
["classic-maya","Classic Maya","the Classic Maya",0,[["K'uhul ajaw (Holy lords and overlords)","kaloomte' (overlord)",1,"Each kingdom was ruled by a holy lord, the k’uhul ajaw, who stood between his people and the gods.","Each Maya kingdom had a “holy lord”, a king who led its rituals and its wars.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kinich-janaab-pakal.jpg",1,"Sergio; derivative work by TastyCakes · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PacalII.svg"],["Ajaw (Nobles)","sajal (\"feared one\", a subordinate lord), ch'ok (\"youth\", a young noble)",2,"Below the kings stood a hereditary nobility of royal kin and great families.","Maya nobles lived in stone houses near the centre of their city, wore jade and served at the king’s court.","/images/societies/thumbs/classic-maya-2.jpg",2,"BIOLOGO JORGE AYALA · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LOS_NOBLES_DE_BONAMPAK.jpg"],["Commoners","",3,"The great majority were commoners: farmers, labourers and servants, and also craftsmen and a few minor officials.","Most Maya were farmers who grew maize, beans and squash and lived in houses with thatched roofs.","/images/societies/thumbs/classic-maya-5.jpg",5,"Lugares INAH · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aj_waaj%2C_Maya_woman_offering_a_plate_of_tamales%2C_Chiik_Nahb_murals%2C_Mexico.jpg"],["Baak (Captives and the enslaved)","",4,"Warfare filled the lowest rank with captives.","Prisoners taken in war were made to work for their captors, and some were sacrificed to the gods.","/images/societies/thumbs/classic-maya-6.jpg",6,"Gary Todd · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captive_Figure%2C_Tonina%2C_Late_Classic%2C_600-900_AD.jpg"]]],
["aztec-empire","Aztecs","the Aztec Empire",1,[["Huey tlatoani (The emperor)","",1,"At the top stood the huey tlatoani, the “great speaker”.","The huey tlatoani, or “great speaker”, ruled the Aztecs from the island city of Tenochtitlan.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huey-tlatoani.jpg",1,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nezahualpiltzintli.jpg"],["Pipiltin (Nobles)","teteuctin (lords)",2,"The nobles (pipiltin) were born to their rank and claimed descent from the Toltecs and, through them, from the god Quetzalcoatl.","Aztec nobles were born into their rank, went to temple schools and became generals, officials and priests.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacaelel.jpg",2,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tlacochcalcatl.jpg"],["Macehualtin (Commoners)","",5,"Most people were commoners (macehualtin), organised in calpolli: wards or villages of related families, each with its own leader, temple and school.","Most Aztecs were farmers who grew maize, beans and squash and gave part of what they made to their rulers.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/macehualtin.jpg",5,"Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacehualChurubuscoDF.JPG"],["Tlacotin (Enslaved people)","",7,"Enslaved people (tlacotin) were owned by others, but Aztec slavery was not inherited: the children of slaves were born free.","Enslaved people had to work for an owner without pay, but their children were born free.","/images/societies/thumbs/aztec-empire-7.jpg",7,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collared.jpg"]]],
["haudenosaunee","the Haudenosaunee","the Haudenosaunee Confederacy",0,[["Clan mothers","-",1,"The clan mothers, the elder women of each clan, chose its chiefs.","Clan mothers were respected older women who chose the chiefs of their clans and could remove them.","/images/societies/thumbs/haudenosaunee-1.jpg",1,"Unknown author · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Untitled%2C_Haudenosaunee_(Iroquois)_Woman-saam_2021.20.jpg"],["Pine tree chiefs and war chiefs","Pinetree Chiefs, War Chiefs",2,"Below the League chiefs stood two kinds of leader who could not vote in council: the pine tree chiefs, who served as diplomats, and the war chiefs, who led the war parties.","War chiefs led war parties, and other chiefs travelled as messengers to talk with other nations.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/joseph-brant.jpg",3,"Gilbert Stuart · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Brant_2.jpg"],["Warriors","-",3,"War was a young man’s road to standing.","Young men went to war to prove their courage, often to bring back captives rather than to kill.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/beaver-wars.jpg",5,"Scan by NYPL · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champlain%27s_Battle_with_the_Iroquois,_Ticonderoga,_July,_1609_(NYPL_b12647398-79471)_(cropped).tiff"],["Adopted captives","-",4,"The mourning wars brought captives into the villages to replace the dead.","Captives taken in war were often adopted into families to take the place of people who had died.","/images/societies/thumbs/haudenosaunee-7.jpg",7,"Unknown author · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Long_House_Iroquois_Allen.jpg"],["Gatsennen (Captives kept as slaves)","",5,"Not every captive was adopted.","Some captives were not adopted and had to work for the village, or were traded away.","/images/societies/thumbs/haudenosaunee-8.jpg",8,"Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iroquois_club-on_display_2-IMG_9609-black.jpg"]]],
["saint-domingue","Saint-Domingue","Saint-Domingue",0,[["Gouverneur général (The governor-general and the intendant)","intendant",1,"Saint-Domingue, the richest colony of the French crown, was governed for the king by two officials sent from France.","A governor and an intendant sent from France ran Saint-Domingue for the king.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/saint-domingue.jpg",1,"Bluebear2 · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavillon_LouisXIV.svg"],["Grands blancs (Great planters and merchants)","",2,"At the top of colonial society stood the grands blancs, the “big whites”: the owners of the great sugar estates, the leading merchants of Cap-Français and Port-au-Prince, and the senior officials…","The richest white planters owned huge sugar farms, and many of them lived far away in France.","/images/societies/thumbs/saint-domingue-2.jpg",2,"James Sharples · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mederic-Louis-Elie_Moreau_de_Saint-Mery_MET_DT203407.jpg"],["Petits blancs (Poorer whites)","",3,"Most whites were petits blancs, “little whites”: overseers and managers on the plantations, artisans, shopkeepers, clerks, sailors, soldiers, slave dealers and day labourers, along with small…","Poorer white people worked as overseers, craftsmen and shopkeepers, and many of them died of fevers.","/images/societies/thumbs/saint-domingue-3.jpg",3,"Nicolas Ponce, after Nicolas Ozanne · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_du_Cap-Fran%C3%A7ais_(Nicolas_Ozanne_et_Nicolas_Ponce).png"],["Gens de couleur libres (Free people of colour)","affranchis",4,"Saint-Domingue had the largest and richest free population of colour in the Caribbean.","Free people of colour could own farms and businesses, but unfair laws kept them out of many jobs.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/toussaint-louverture.jpg",4,"Pierre-Charles Baquoy (1759-1829) · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toussaint_Louverture_par_Pierre-Charles_Baquoy.jpg"],["Esclaves de jardin (Enslaved field workers)","bossales (born in Africa)",6,"The great majority of Saint-Domingue’s people were enslaved field workers, and about two-thirds of them had been born in Africa.","Most people in Saint-Domingue were enslaved and forced to work on sugar and coffee farms without pay.","/images/societies/thumbs/saint-domingue-6.jpg",6,"William Clark · CC0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slaves_cutting_the_sugar_cane_-_Ten_Views_in_the_Island_of_Antigua_(1823)%2C_plate_IV_-_BL.jpg"]]],
["hawaii","Hawaii","Hawaii under the kapu",0,[["Aliʻi nui (The paramount chief)","mōʻī",1,"Each island kingdom had its paramount chief (aliʻi nui), the greatest of the chiefs in power, if not always in birth.","Kamehameha I was a great chief who united the Hawaiian Islands and became their first king.","/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kalaniopuu.jpg",1,"Mark Miller · CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalani%CA%BB%C5%8Dpu%CA%BBu_%CA%BBAhu_%CA%BBula_and_mahiole.jpg"],["Aliʻi (High chiefs)","aliʻi ʻaimoku (district chiefs), nīʻaupiʻo (the highest grade)",2,"The chiefs (aliʻi) traced their descent from the gods, and a chief’s rank depended on the genealogy of both parents.","High chiefs wore cloaks made from many thousands of tiny red and yellow bird feathers.","/images/societies/thumbs/hawaii-2.jpg",2,"John Webber · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Webber's_oil_painting_'A_Chief_of_the_Sandwich_Islands'%2C_1787.jpg"],["Makaʻāinana (Commoners)","",4,"Most Hawaiians were commoners (makaʻāinana), who farmed, fished and practised the simpler crafts.","Most Hawaiians were farmers and fishers who grew taro, caught fish and gave part of their food to the chiefs.","/images/societies/thumbs/hawaii-5.jpg",5,"Joseph Strong · Public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_with_a_Yoke_Carrying_Taro_by_Joseph_Strong%2C_oil_on_canvas_board%2C_1880%2C_Honolulu_Museum_of_Art%2C_accession_12692.1.JPG"],["Kauwā (Outcasts)","",5,"At the bottom stood the kauwā, a hereditary group set apart from every other class and despised by it.","The kauwā were outcasts: other Hawaiians were forbidden to marry them, and they worked for the chiefs.","/images/societies/thumbs/hawaii-6.jpg",6,"Mike SEAGER THOMAS · CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mo'okini_heiau.jpg"]]]
];
